**Read:** Revisit the complete primary works supporting the final argument and their corrections or follow-up studies. Use the earlier metrology and reproducible-computation Records as needed.

Present the question, prior work, assumptions, methods, evidence, independent checks, uncertainty and limits as one argument. Make calculations and source locations inspectable. Distinguish published results from your own observations and synthetic examples. Preserve negative results and failed hypotheses when they constrain the conclusion.

Invite a technically informed reader to challenge the strongest claim, the hardest dependency and a plausible alternative explanation. Answer with evidence or narrow the claim. Revision is part of scholarship; a polished report does not substitute for a result that survives scrutiny.

**Work:** Produce a sustained research report and a concise oral or written defense. Rework it after criticism. Identify one result another person can independently reproduce and one open question whose resolution would materially improve the service account.

